+A project's repository URL, lockfiles, package names, vulnerability titles and release feeds can all be attacker-shaped. The scan pipeline contains what such input can do to its own run: repository URLs validate against a host allowlist with credential and control-character refusals and never begin with `-` (the SSH `account@host:path` form is matched as a whole, so no part of it reads as a git option or transport), every git command places `--` before the URL so git reads it as a repository whatever it holds, clones run in a fixed scrubbed environment with redirects disabled and positional (non-option) refs only, lockfile parsing reads at most the configured bytes per file and caps traversed and matched files, feeds screen entity declarations across the whole bounded payload and the parser itself refuses a document type or entity declaration in any encoding (a UTF-16 feed spells one in bytes the screen does not match), one malformed lockfile or finding costs that item alone, and display values are cleaned before storage. Within those limits hostile input can still describe false dependencies or unsafe-looking findings; the catalogue marks their provenance through the recorded source files and commit, and reviewers or adopters verify before asserting anything downstream.
 
 ## Authentication
 
+### Pages
+
+nginx serves a page only to a signed-in browser, as KEEN's does: each page request is first put to the API (`auth_request` to `/v1/auth/check`), so a signed-out browser receives the sign-in page in place of the page it asked for (status 403, at the same address, which a completed sign-in returns to) and never the application's own markup. The administration page and its scripts are checked against `/v1/auth/check-admin` and answer 404 to any other account. The sign-in, second-factor and e-mail confirmation pages, scripts and styles are served without a session; none holds account data. The gate keeps pages from signed-out browsers; every record is still authorised by the API. The second-factor page continues only to OSPMAE's own pages: like the sign-in page, it refuses a destination containing a backslash or a control character, which a browser would read as another site.
+
 ### Sessions
 
 Browser sessions use random, server-generated identifiers. The database (`sessions`) keeps only the SHA-256 digest of each identifier, as KEEN's session store does, so the table, a backup or a database reader holds no identifier a browser could present; every lookup of a presented identifier goes through `security/sessions.session_row`. The browser receives an HttpOnly session cookie with `SameSite` policy and `Secure` flag from configuration; CSRF protection pairs a separate CSRF cookie with a header check written by `security/csrf.py`. Where cookies are Secure and no cookie domain is set, the session, CSRF, device, pending second-factor and SSO binding cookies take the `__Host-` prefix (`__Host-ospmae_session`, `__Host-ospmae_csrf`), which a browser accepts only from the installation's own host over HTTPS, so a site on a sibling subdomain can neither set nor shadow them. A session ends at its sliding expiry and also at a fixed time after sign-in (`OSPMAE_SESSION_ABSOLUTE_TTL_SECONDS`, 30 days by default), however recently it was used. Signing in issues a fresh identifier; signing out revokes the account's sessions.
